 { White is more active, but who would guess that the battle would be over in just ten moves? A key role was played by Alekhine's next, powerful move. } 1. { -KEY- } Bd3 $1 { Provoking the advance of Black's h-pawn, so that it can be more easily attacked. } h6 { The least of evils. } ( { a) After } 1... Nxc3 $6 2. Bxh7 { the white h-pawn becomes a giant. } ) 
 ( { b) And } 1... h5 2. Bf5 ( { less clear is } 1... h6 1. Bg6 Nxc3 3. Bxh5 f5 $1 { preparing ...Ne4; } ) 
Kd8 3. g4 $1 Ne7 4. Bb1 { has a similar effect. } ) 
2. Bf5+ Kd8 3. Kg4 $1 { Active king, with focus on the h6-pawn. White's own pawn on c3 is less of a concern. } Ne7 ( { Or } 3... Nxc3 4. Kh5 Nxa4 5. Kxh6 { and the h-pawn decides. } ) 
4. Bb1 Ke8 $6 ( 4... Rd5 { was more stubborn, cutting off the enemy king from the fifth rank. There could follow } 5. Re6 $5 { (more provocation) } f5 6. Kh5 Rd1 7. Ba2 Rd3 8. Kxh6 Rxg3 9. h5 { and White keeps the initiative. } ) 
5. Kh5 $1 { The plan initiated four moves ago is completed. } Kf7 6. Ba2+ $1 ( { Simpler than } 6. Kxh6 $6 f5 7. Kh5 Kf6 { intending ...Rd8-h8. } ) 
Kf8 ( { Obviously, } 6... Nd5 $2 { failed to } 7. c4 { and 8.c5+ next. } ) 
7. Kxh6 Rd2 ( 7... f5 8. Kh5 Rd3 { was slightly more tenacious, although after } 9. g4 fxg4 10. Kxg4 Rxc3 11. h5 { White should win anyway. } ) 
8. Be6 Rd3 9. g4 Rxc3 10. g5 { In this hopeless position, Black resigned. } 
