 { Thanks to his extra pawn and superior minor piece, Black is clearly better. With my next move, I provoke a concession in the enemy kingside before placing my bishop on d4. } 1... { -KEY- } Bg1 $1 { Forcing the white h-pawn to approach Black's king, in similar fashion to the previous example. } 2. h3 Bd4 ( { There was in fact nothing wrong with the straightforward } 2... Kg6 3. Nd5 Kg5 { but I wanted to restrict the enemy knight first. } ) 
3. b3 Rb6 ( { Preparing to activate the rook via a6-a1 whenever White moves the knight. In any case, } 3... Kg6 { was also possible, for example, } 4. Nd5 Kg5 5. f4 Kh4 $1 { or } 4. Ne2 Kg5 $1 5. Nxd4 exd4 6. Rxd4 Kh4 { when Black soon recovers the pawn, with a crushing positional advantage. } ) 
4. Ne2 Ra6+ 5. Kb1 ( { Or } 5. Ra4 Rxa4 6. bxa4 Bf2 { with ...Kg6-g5-h4 next. } ) 
Ra1+ 6. Kc2 Ra2+ 7. Kd3 Bb6 8. Rc6 $2 { This won't end well, but on other moves Black decides the battle by a king transfer to h4. } Rb2 $1 ( { Seeing that Black wins a second pawn after } 8... Rb2 9. Rc3 ( { or } 8... Rb2 8. Nc1 Rf2 ) 
Ba5 { White resigned. } ) 

