 { It is easy to see that the c-file will be a key factor in this endgame. Before starting to work on it, Karpov applied a subtle provocative measure. } 1. { -KEY- } Ba3 $1 { Provoking the advance ...b5-b4, so that this pawn settles on a square of the same colour as White's bishop, while the c4-square is freed for the white forces. } ( { It is important to effect this move before the enemy king joins the battle; i.e. } 1. Rc1 $6 Kf7 2. Ba3 ( { just as after } 2. Rc6 Rxc6 3. dxc6 Rc8 4. Rc1 Ke6 ) 
Ke7 { and Black defends - Karpov. } ) 
b4 ( { a) Practically forced; since } 1... Rd8 $6 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1... Rb6 $6 { permit } 2. Rc1 { seizing the open file. } ) 
2. Bb2 Kf7 3. Rc1 Nf4+ { Evidently, the knight must join the battle as well. } 4. Ke3 g5 5. Bd4 $1 { An ideal square for the bishop, where it controls the whole board. } Ke7 6. Rc6 $1 { Now this is indeed strong - in contrast to the 1.Rc1?! subline above, White has a target on b4 to work on. } Rxc6 7. dxc6 Rc8 8. Rc1 Ne6 9. Bb6 $1 { The strong bishop should stay on the board. } ( { After } 9. Rc4 $6 Nc5 $1 10. Bxc5 dxc5 11. Rxc5 Kd6 12. Rf5 Ke6 { Black holds - Karpov. } ) 
Nc5 10. c7 Ne6 ( { Karpov also indicates the line } 10... Kd7 11. Kd4 Ne6 12. Kd5 Nxc7 13. Rxc7 Rxc7 14. Bxc7 Kxc7 15. Ke6 { and White wins. } ) 
11. Rc4 Kd7 12. Rxb4 Nxc7 { The trade of the b- and d-pawns produced a white pawn majority on the queenside, which could soon turn into an outside passed pawn. Thus Karpov is happy the more pieces are exchanged off the board. Besides, this will help him to reduce enemy counterplay. } 13. Rc4 $1 Ne8 ( { The more active } 13... Ne6 { permits } 14. Rxc8 Kxc8 15. Bd4 $1 Nxd4 16. Kxd4 { and the outside passed pawn decides. } ) 
14. Rxc8 Kxc8 15. Kd4 Kb7 16. Ba5 Kc6 17. Kc4 Kd7 18. Bc3 $1 { Tying the knight to the defence of the f6-pawn. } h5 19. a4 ( { The immediate } 19. Bd4 { was possible as well. } ) 
Ke6 20. Bd4 $1 ( { a) The obvious } 20. b4 $6 { would permit } Nc7 21. b5 axb5 22. axb5 d5 $1 { with counterplay - Karpov. } ) 
 ( { b) Whereas after } 20. Bd4 Nc7 { runs into } 21. Bb6 $1 { with the idea } d5 $2 22. Kd4 { So Illescas looks for activity on the opposite flank. } ) 
f5 21. exf5+ Kxf5 22. Kd5 $1 { The king exploits the moment to get closer to the queenside. } Kf4 23. b4 Kxf3 24. Kc6 $1 { The magic distance. Now the b-pawn becomes unstoppable. } g4 25. b5 axb5 26. a5 $5 ( 26. axb5 h4 27. b6 g3 28. h3 { was equally strong. } ) 
Ke4 ( { The point behind 26.a5 is revealed after } 26... h4 27. a6 g3 28. a7 ( { though } 27... Ke4 27. h3 { works here as well } ) 
gxh2 29. a8=Q $11 h1=Q $11 30. Kd7 ) 
27. a6 
 1-0

