 { Provocation also becomes an important weapon when possessing the bishop pair, as can be seen in the next, famous example.In exchange for the pawn deficit White has a powerful bishop pair, while Black is struggling with his passive knights. Moreover, the fact that there is play on both flanks favours the bishops, as can be seen by Botvinnik's next, simple move. } 1. { -KEY- } Bf4 $1 ( { Provoking the advance of the h-pawn, so that it becomes more vulnerable. A different move order with } 1. exd5 exd5 2. Bf4 { also works, as noticed by Neilthesculptor. } ) 
h5 2. exd5 $1 { At the right moment, ruling out any attempts at ...d5xe4, followed by ...Ne7-d5 - Botvinnik. } exd5 3. h4 $1 { Fixing the h-pawn for the light-squared bishop, and creating a stronghold on g5 for its colleague. } Nab8 4. Bg5+ Kf7 5. Bf5 { Preventing ...Nd7 and eyeing c8. } Na7 ( { After } 5... Ne7 6. Bh3 $1 ( 5... Na7 5. Bxe7 Kxe7 7. Bg6 { is somewhat less technical due to the risk of a theoretical draw with the h-pawn and the wrong bishop; } ) 
Nbc6 7. Bg2 Kg7 8. Bxe7 $1 Nxe7 9. Kb4 Nc6 10. Kb5 Nxd4 11. Kxb6 { the a-pawn soon decides. } ) 
6. Bf4 Nbc6 7. Bd3 Nc8 8. Be2 Kg6 9. Bd3+ Kf6 10. Be2 Kg6 11. Bf3 N6e7 ( { On } 11... N8e7 { a good reply is } 12. Bc7 Nf5 13. Bxd5 Nfxd4 14. Bxb6 { - Botvinnik. } ) 
12. Bg5 $1 { Finding himself in a fatal zugzwang, Black resigned. } ( { If } 12. Bg5 Nc6 { then } 13. Bxd5 Nd6 14. Bf3 Kf5 15. Bc1 $1 { (Smyslov) is very strong, avoiding a fork on e4; e.g. } b5 16. Bxc6 bxc6 17. a5 { and White wins. } ) 

 1-0

