 { Thanks to his more active king White is better, but this advantage could quickly evaporate if Black achieves ...b7-b6+, followed by ...Kd6. } 1. { -KEY- } Ng5 $1 { A key move that softens up Black's kingside pawns for a later attack, by the knight itself or even by the king. } f6 ( { Here or later, } 1... b6 2. Kd4 { makes no great difference. } ) 
2. Nh7 f5 3. h4 $1 { Fixing a weakness on g6. } ( { It is important to use the h-pawn for this purpose: after } 3. f4 $2 { the king can no longer use the c1-h6 diagonal, and Black holds after } b6 4. Kd4 Kd6 5. Nf8 Nc6 6. Ke3 Ne7 { - Dvoretsky. } ) 
f4 ( { Kholmov opts for active defence, aware that after } 3... b6 4. Kd4 Kd6 5. Nf8 Nc6 6. Ke3 Ne5 7. Kf4 { the g6-pawn can't be saved - Botvinnik. } ) 
4. Nf8 b6+ ( { Perhaps } 4... f3 $5 5. g3 Nf5 6. Nxg6 Nxg3 { offered more practical chances, although after } 7. Kd4 { White soon ends up with an extra pawn anyway - Botvinnik. } ) 
5. Kd4 Nf5+ ( { It no longer makes sense to play } 5... f3 6. g3 Nf5 { in view of } 7. Kd3 $1 { ruling out the ... Nxg3 sacrifice. } ) 
6. Ke4 Nxh4 ( { On this occasion, } 6... f3 { fails to } 7. Kxf3 Nxh4 8. Kg3 { e.g. } Nf5 ( { or } 8... g5 9. Ne6 ) 
9. Kf4 { - Botvinnik. } ) 
7. Ne6+ Kc6 8. Nxf4 ( { The threat of } 9. g3 { has emerged, and Black is now forced to try his luck on the opposite flank. } ) 
Kb5 ( { According to Botvinnik, when playing 4...b6+ Kholmov had overlooked that } 8... g5 { at this point runs into } 9. g3 $1 gxf4 10. gxh4 { and White wins the pawn ending. } ) 
9. g3 Nf5 10. Nxg6 Nh6 11. Ne5 $1 { A final accuracy, assigning the knight to the queenside defence. } Ka4 12. Nc4 Kb3 ( { a) The pawn trade won't favour Black; but on } 12... Kb5 { White builds a decisive barrier by } 13. Nb2 ) 
 ( { b) While after } 12... b5 13. Ne5 Kxa3 14. Nc6 $1 { the knight defends the pawn comfortably from the front, a topic that we came across in Dvoretsky's study earlier in this chapter -Example 247. } ) 
13. Nxb6 Kxa3 14. Nd5 Kb3 15. f4 Kc4 16. Nc7 Kxb4 17. Nxa6+ 
