 { White is slightly better in this typical IQP endgame, thanks to the eternal weakness on d5. On the other hand, a single weakness usually isn't enough for victory in the endgame; besides, in such a structure Black is happy to have a knight on the board rather than a bishop. White has just played Kg1-f1, activating his king; how should Black react? } 1... { -KEY- } Rxc1+ $2 { Quite a common endgame mistake: swapping pieces in the belief that the defensive task is made easier by such action. As we will see next, Black's king will soon be missing in the battle. } ( { For this reason, } 1... Kg7 { followed by ...Kf6, was preferable, activating his own king while awaiting the opponent's next step. } ) 
2. Rxc1 Rc8 3. Rxc8+ Nxc8 4. Ne2 $1 { The knight hurries to attack the enemy pawns before Black's king joins the battle. } ( { Less promising is } 4. Ke2 $2 Kf8 5. Kd3 Ke7 6. Ne2 Kd6 { when } 7. Kd4 $2 { can be met by } Ne7 { heading for c6 - Seirawan. } ) 
Kg7 ( { After } 4... Kf8 $6 5. Nc3 Nb6 6. Ke2 ( { another idea is } 5... Kg7 5. a4 $5 a5 7. Ke2 Ke7 8. Kd3 Kd6 9. Kd4 { - Seirawan. } ) 
Ke7 7. Kd3 Kd6 8. Kd4 { Black is in serious trouble: his knight is badly placed at the magic distance from the enemy king, and White has a strong plan with b3-b4 and a2-a4-a5. With the text Adianto plans instead to place his knight on the more flexible e7-square. } ) 
5. Nc3 { The right route, approaching the black queenside pawns. } ( { Less promising is } 5. Nf4 $6 Ne7 6. Ke2 Kf6 7. Kd3 Ke5 { when the white knight has no interesting prospects from f4. } ) 
Ne7 6. Na4 $1 ( { Again Seirawan takes the chance to attack the enemy pawns before the black king enters the stage. Now the threat of } 7. Nc5 { forces Black's reply. } ) 
b5 7. Nc5 a5 8. Nb7 $1 { In the spirit of provoking weaknesses. } Nc6 ( { On } 8... a4 { Seirawan advocates } 9. bxa4 ( { instead, White could continue } 8... Nc6 8. Nd6 { as in the game; e.g. } axb3 10. axb3 b4 11. Ne8 $1 Kf8 12. Nc7 Kg7 13. Ke2 Kf6 14. f4 $1 { preventing ...Ke5 while preparing g2-g4 and Kd3-d4 } ) 
bxa4 10. a3 { fixing the a4-pawn as a target, though after } Nc8 $5 { followed by ...Nb6, things might not be completely clear. } ) 
9. Nd6 $1 { Not so much to attack the b5-pawn but to reach e8, from where the knight will restrict the enemy king and also access an excellent post on f6. } b4 10. Ne8+ Kf8 { Black gives up a pawn in order to get his king to the centre. } ( { The odd } 10... Kh6 { might have been more tenacious, upon which Seirawan suggests } 11. Ke2 Kg5 12. Nc7 $1 d4 13. Nb5 $1 dxe3 14. Kxe3 { preparing Ke4, with good winning chances. } ) 
11. Nf6 d4 $1 { Active defence. } ( { After } 11... Ke7 $6 12. Nxd5 Ke6 13. Nb6 { White wins with less effort. } ) 
12. Nxh7+ Ke7 13. Ng5 $1 { The knight escapes before it is trapped by ...f7-f6, and completes a journey of ten moves in a row! As I mentioned earlier in this chapter, in the endgame this piece should often move around a lot. } dxe3 14. fxe3 Ne5 { A good square for the knight, where it protects the f7-pawn and temporarily restricts White's king. } ( { The optimistic } 14... Na7 { heading for b5-c3, fails to } 15. Ke2 Nb5 16. Kd3 Nc3 17. a4 $1 { etc - Seirawan. } ) 
15. Ke2 Kd6 16. Nf3 $1 { Thus the king gets access to d3, after which White's extra pawn will start to make itself felt. } Nd7 17. Kd3 Nb6 18. e4 f6 19. Kd4 { A natural centralization. } ( { Seirawan also suggests } 19. h4 $5 { in order to create an outside passed pawn by g2-g4 and h4-h5. } ) 
a4 { It is hard to suggest anything better. } 20. Ne1 ( { White now had a good choice in } 20. e5 fxe5 21. Nxe5 { but Seirawan prefers a more technical path. } ) 
axb3 21. axb3 Nd7 22. Nd3 { Establishing a barrier. } ( { Of course not } 22. Kc4 $2 Ke5 $1 { with counterplay - Seirawan. } ) 
Nf8 23. h4 $1 { Creating some kind of zugzwang. } ( { a) } 23. Nxb4 Ne6 24. Ke3 ( { or } 24. Kc4 Nf4 ) 
Nc5 { would be less technical. } ) 
 ( { b) After } 23. h4 { Black resigned, in view of } Ne6 24. Kc4 { and White wins another pawn. } ) 

 1-0

