 { Provocation is a common guest in rook endings as well. Here are some examples.Thanks to his more active rook and king, White is a bit better, but Black holds for the moment. Rubinstein finds a great idea. } 1. { -KEY- } Rh3 $1 { Softening up Black's kingside. } ( { The move order matters: after } 1. Rg3 $6 g6 2. Rh3 Rc7 { Black defends better than in the game. } ) 
h6 ( { After } 1... g6 2. Rxh7 Rxc3 3. Ra7 $1 { White obtains a big advantage, as demonstrated by Marin's detailed analysis in 'Learn from the Legends'. One sample line is } Rc6 4. Rg7 $1 Kf6 5. Rb7 Ke6 6. h4 $1 { with } -- 7. Rb6 { next. } ) 
2. Rg3 $1 { In the same provocative spirit. } Kf6 ( { a) The king has to cede the d5-square to its counterpart, since both } 2... Rc7 3. Rg6 ) 
 ( { b) And } 2... g5 3. Rh3 $1 { lose a pawn for Black. } ) 
3. Kd5 Re6 4. Rf3+ Ke7 { Thanks to the previous provocation White has managed to invade with his king, and Black is evidently on the defensive. But how to progress? } 5. g3 $1 { Aware of Black's potential counterplay with ...Rd6-d2, Rubinstein takes prophylactic measures. The creation of a passed pawn with c3-c4 can wait until a more opportune moment - the opponent can't undertake anything active anyway. } ( { a) Instead, the tempting } 5. c4 $6 { permits } bxc4 6. bxc4 Rd6 $1 7. Kxe5 Rd2 8. Rg3 g5 { with quite some activity for Black; } ) 
 ( { b) Even worse is } 5. Rf5 $2 Rd6 $1 6. Kxe5 Rd2 { and the g2-pawn falls. } ) 
 ( { c) But } 5. Re3 { is the engine's choice, based on the variation } Rd6 $1 6. Kxe5 Rd2 7. Rg3 $1 { (in the provocative spirit of this chapter) } g5 8. Rh3 Rxg2 9. Rxh6 { when Black unfortunately loses the a6-pawn as well. Even so, I'd prefer Rubinstein's choice for practical reasons - Black's counterplay is limited and there is less need for calculation, which is a welcome fact when you are beyond move 40 or so, considering time and fatigue factors. } ) 
g6 ( { Preventing } 5... g6 5. Rf5 ) 
 ( { If } 5... Rf6 { with the same purpose, then } 6. Re3 $1 Rf2 7. Rxe5 Kd7 8. Kc5 $1 { followed by Kb6 and the a-pawn soon takes its toll. } ) 
 ( { Perhaps Black's best try was the sacrifice } 5... e4 $5 6. Re3 Rd6 7. Kxe4 ( { or } 6... g6 6. Kc5 Re6 ) 
Rd2 { , as suggested by FSU70, with more counterplay than in the game thanks to the active rook. } ) 
6. Re3 $1 { Before commencing the advance of the c-pawn, White eliminates the ...Rd6+ resource by the threat against e5. } Kf6 7. c4 bxc4 8. bxc4 { The c-pawn has become a giant, and the rest is child's play for Rubinstein. } Re8 9. Rf3+ { Driving the enemy king away from the defence of the e-pawn. } Kg5 10. c5 e4 11. h4+ ( { The simple } 11. Re3 { was strong too, both here and on the next move. } ) 
Kg4 12. Rf4+ Kxg3 13. Rxe4 Ra8 ( { Or } 13... Rd8 14. Kc6 { followed by } -- 15. Kb7 { - Marin. } ) 
14. c6 g5 15. hxg5 hxg5 16. c7 Kf3 17. Re1 { Black resigned, seeing that after the trade of rooks and passed pawns, the location of White's king decides. Note that this position would be drawn without the a-pawns - not without reason, a greater number of pawns on the board tends to favour the attacker. } 
 1-0

