 { White has an extra pawn, but the enemy rook is quite active on the second rank. Kramnik found an interesting way of activating his own rook. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra3 $1 { Softening up Black's queenside pawns. } ( 1. Rd3 $6 Ke6 { was less effective. } ) 
Rxb2 { One of several unsatisfactory options. } ( { a) If } 1... a5 { then } 2. Rb3 { in similar fashion to Example 289 (Rubinstein-Cohn), when after } Rc6 3. Rb5 { preparing f4-f5, White's extra pawn and more active pieces imply a decisive advantage. } ) 
 ( { b) After } 1... Rc7 2. b4 { Black is also in trouble - Kramnik; e.g. } Rd7 3. Rc3 { heading for c6. } ) 
2. Rxa7+ Kg8 ( { Unfortunately for Black, the active } 2... Ke6 { fails to } 3. Rg7 Kf5 4. Rf7 Ke6 5. Rf6 ) 
3. f5 $1 { Of course. This advance clears a path for the king via f4-e5 and creates a strong passed pawn. } Rb5 ( { More stubborn than } 3... gxf5 4. Kf4 Rb5 5. g6 { - Kramnik. } ) 
4. Ra8+ Kf7 ( { Or } 4... Kg7 5. f6 { with the strong plan of Ra7+, f3-f4 and Rg7. } ) 
5. fxg6+ Kxg6 6. f4 Rb1 7. Rg8+ Kf5 ( { White also wins after } 7... Kh7 8. Rc8 Rg1 9. Kf3 h4 10. Rc6 { - Kramnik. } ) 
8. Rf8+ Ke4 9. Re8+ Kf5 10. Re5+ Kg6 11. Re6+ Kf5 12. Rf6+ { White has finally got the rook where he wanted and can now start advancing his pair of connected passed pawns. } Ke4 13. g6 b5 14. Kh4 ( { Another good option was } 14. f5 { preparing Rf7 and g6-g7, in similar fashion to Example 65 (Szabo-Keres) in Chapter Two. There could follow } Ra1 15. Rf7 h4 16. Kg2 $1 ( { not } 16. Kxh4 $2 Kf4 $1 ) 
Rxa2 17. Kh3 Ra3 18. Kxh4 { and 19.g7 decides next move. } ) 
Rh1+ 15. Kg5 h4 16. Re6+ Kf3 17. g7 Rg1+ 18. Kf6 h3 ( { Or } 18... Kxf4 19. Rb6 h3 20. Rxb5 h2 21. Rh5 { etc. } ) 
19. Re8 ( { In view of } 19. Re8 h2 20. Rh8 { Black resigned. } ) 

