 { Again White is a pawn up, but two pawns stop three on the kingside. Smyslov found a hidden opportunity on the opposite flank. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra8 $1 { Provoking a weak point on b6, which can be used later on by his majesty. } a6 ( { In the event of } 1... b6 $2 { one refutation is } 2. Re6 Rf6 3. Rxf6 Kxf6 4. Rc8 ) 
2. Re6+ { Before reactivating the rook on a8, White centralizes his other rook again. } Rf6 3. Re5 Rf4 4. Rd8 Rf6 ( { On the more active } 4... Rc4 { White could reply } 5. Kd2 { preparing 6.Re4. } ) 
5. Kd4 $1 { The time is right to send the king to b6. } Rf4+ 6. Kc3 Kf6 7. Ree8 a5 8. Kb3 $1 { Smyslov sticks to his plan. } ( { In contrast, the alternative } 8. Re4 $2 { was no longer beneficial, due to } Rxe4 9. fxe4 axb4 { and ...Ke5 next. } ) 
axb4 9. axb4 Kg6 10. Rd6+ Rf6 11. Rd2 ( 11. Rxf6 $6 Kxf6 12. Ka4 { was less technical due to } Kf5 $1 { , copying the opponent's plan on the opposite flank. } ) 
Kf5 12. Rd4 $1 { A key move that prevents ...Kf4 and aims at deploying the rook on g4, where it will defend both of White's more vulnerable pawns. As a consequence, the exchange of the other rook is no longer a concern for him. } Re6 13. Rxe6 Kxe6 14. Ka4 Ke5 15. Rg4 Kf6 16. f4 $1 ( { Black resigned, in view of } 16. f4 gxf4 17. Rxg7 Kxg7 18. Ka5 { and the king finally reaches b6 with a trivial win - Smyslov. } ) 

