 { Provocative measures with the rook are often seen in other types of endings as well. Here is one example.Black is slightly more active here and the bishop outranks the knight. Even so, it is not easy to find a way to progress at first glance. } 1... { -KEY- } Rf5 $1 { A clever provocative move. The threat of 2...Bc4 now forces White to play g3-g4, after which his kingside pawns will be fixed on squares of the same colour as the bishop; besides, the f4-square is softened up for the black rook. } ( { Not } 1... Rf3 $4 2. Rxd5 ) 
2. g4 ( { Indeed, most other moves have clear defects; e.g. } 2. Kd2 $6 Rf3 ) 
Rf7 { Suddenly White is short of solid moves. } 3. Ne1 Rf4 $1 ( { Salov at once exploits the weakness on f4; aware that } 3... Rf4 4. Rxf4 $6 gxf4 { , followed by ...Kc4-b3, would just make things worse for White - Ribli. } ) 
4. Ke3 Rf1 $1 { Looking for new targets. } ( { It was no longer beneficial to swap rooks; i.e. } 4... Rxd4 $2 5. Kxd4 { and White manages to activate his king. } ) 
5. Nc2 $1 ( { a) A good defence; as } 5. Nc2 { creates the threat of } -- 6. c4 Bxc4 7. Na3 ) 
 ( { b) Inferior is } 5. Ke2 $2 Rh1 { and White loses a pawn in view of } 6. Rd3 $2 Bc4 ) 
Rf3+ 6. Kd2 ( { a) Not } 6. Ke2 $2 Rxh3 7. c4 Bxc4 { with check. } ) 
 ( { b) Whereas after } 6. Kd2 { the h3-pawn is untouchable due to } Rxh3 $2 7. c4 Bxc4 8. Na3 { again. } ) 
Bb3 $1 7. Rb4+ $1 { By a little combination White manages to exchange the active enemy rook, thus preserving the material balance, at least for a few more moves. } axb4 8. Nd4+ Kc4 9. Nxf3 bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Kd5 { Despite the departure of the rooks, White's task remains difficult due to the multiple weaknesses on c3, e5 and h3. } 11. Ke3 Bc4 12. h4 ( { a) White was in zugzwang; e.g. } 12. Kf2 Ke4 13. Nd2 Kd3 { and the c3-pawn falls. } ) 
 ( { b) Or } 12. Nd2 Ba6 13. Nf3 Bf1 14. Kf2 Bd3 $1 ( { but not } 14. h4 Bxh3 $2 15. Nh2 $1 ) 
15. Ke3 Bb5 { with a new zugzwang - Hecht. } ) 
gxh4 13. Nxh4 Kxe5 { Black has finally managed to convert his positional assets into a material advantage, and the ensuing endgame can be regarded as technically won. } 14. Nf3+ Kd5 15. Nd2 Bb5 16. Nf3 Ba4 $1 { The bishop has found a new angle on d1. } 17. g5 h5 $1 { As we discussed several times in the book, pawn exchanges usually favour the defender. } 18. Kf4 Bc2 19. Ne5 Bf5 20. Nf3 Kc4 21. Nh4 Bg4 $1 ( { a) Salov avoids both pitfalls: } 21... g6 $2 22. Ke5 Kxc3 23. Kf6 { , heading for a drawn queen ending. } ) 
 ( { b) And } 21... Kxc3 $2 22. Nxf5 exf5 23. Kxf5 Kd4 24. g6 $1 { with a draw - Hecht. } ) 
22. g6 Kxc3 23. Ke5 Kd3 24. Kd6 Ke4 25. Ke7 Kf4 ( { White resigned, seeing that } 25... Kf4 26. Kf8 Kg5 27. Kxg7 Kxh4 28. Kf7 ( { or } 27... Kf4 27. Kh6 e5 ) 
Bf5 { lets Black stop the g-pawn with his bishop. } ) 

 0-1

