 { On occasion, provocation can also be realized by the queen, as in the next example.White has a huge advantage thanks to his sounder pawn structure and more active knight. A simple provocation measure helped me to accelerate the end. } 1. { -KEY- } Qh2 $1 ( { By threatening } 2. Qc7 { ( or } 2. Qd6 { ), White provokes ...e6-e5, thus creating a powerful outpost for the knight on f5 and restricting the enemy queen. } ) 
e5 2. Ng3 Qg6 3. Nf5+ Ke6 4. Qh1 $1 { The queen returns to the first rank, planning to invade the enemy camp via the d-file or the a2-g8 diagonal. } e4 ( { Seeing that the passive } 4... Qg8 5. Qd1 Qf8 6. Qd3 { would be fatal for him, Ostenstad opts for complications. } ) 
5. Qh2 $1 { Simplest, in order to exploit the reopened h2-b8 diagonal. } ( { Alternatively, } 5. fxe4 { was also strong; e.g. } Nf6 6. Qd1 Nxe4 7. Kf3 $1 { when both } Nf6 ( { and } 7. Qh2 Ke5 8. Qa1 ) 
8. Qd6 { lead to an easy win. } ) 
exf3+ 6. exf3 Kd7 7. Kf2 $1 { Prophylaxis against a potential check on e2. } ( { a) Less convincing was } 7. Qe5 $6 Qf6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 7. Qb8 Qe6 ) 
Nf8 8. Qe5 Qh7 ( { a) No better is } 8... Ne6 $2 9. Qd6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 8... Qe6 9. Qg7 Ke8 10. Qxg5 ) 
9. Ke3 Ng6 ( { After } 9... Qf7 10. Qd6 Ke8 11. Qb8 Kd7 12. Qb7 { Black loses his queen, as } Ke6 { runs into } 13. Qc6 Ke5 14. Qd6 ) 
10. Qd6+ ( { In view of his hopeless position after } 10. Qd6 Ke8 11. Qxb6 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

