 { So far, the provocative measures were carried out exclusively by the stronger side. The next example shows us that, on occasion, the idea of provocation can also prove useful for the defender.When the bishop fights against three connected pawns, the weaker side can draw only with precise defence. --- } 1... { -KEY- } Bc1 $1 { Thus Black provokes the advance of the pawns to light squares, where they can be controlled more easily by the bishop. } ( { Instead, the game went } 1... Bc3 $2 2. h7 $1 Kg7 ( { or } 2... Bb2 3. f6 ) 
3. Ke6 $1 Kxh7 ( { or } 3... Kh8 4. f6 { followed by Kf7-e8 and wins - Dvoretsky } ) 
4. Kf7 Kh8 5. g6 Bb2 6. f6 { and Black resigned. } ) 
2. h7 ( { Or } 2. g6 Ke7 3. h7 Bb2 { and Black has reached his goal. } ) 
Kg7 3. g6 Bb2 { The bishop now controls all the pawns, though Black should be on his guard against a sacrifice. } 4. Ke6 Kh8 $1 { The only move; } ( { after } 4... Bc3 $2 5. f6 $1 Bxf6 6. h8=Q $1 Kxh8 7. Kxf6 Kg8 8. g7 { White wins - Dvoretsky. } ) 
5. f6 { There is no other way to progress. } Bxf6 6. Kxf6 { Stalemate. } 
 *

