 { Finally, here are a few examples where provocative measures were realized in the presence of more pieces on the board.White enjoys a strong bishop pair and his knight has found a nice post on f5, but what to do next? } 1. { -KEY- } Ba4 $1 { Kotov provokes ...b6-b5 so that the enemy knight loses its retreat square on c5; moreover, the g1-a7 diagonal is cleared for the dark-squared bishop. } ( { Equally interesting was } 1. Rd2 $5 Nhf4 2. Ba4 $1 ( 2. Rad1 Ne6 { promises less; } ) 
b5 3. Bc2 { in the same spirit; e.g. } Ne6 4. Bxd3 Rxd3 5. Rxd3 Rxd3 6. Bxa7 { winning a pawn. } ) 
b5 ( { Practically forced; since after } 1... Rc7 $6 2. Rd2 $1 { followed by Rad1, the pin on the d-file spells trouble for Black - Shereshevsky. } ) 
2. Bb3 Nhf4 3. Rd2 { Having concluded the provocative measure Kotov reverts to normal play, fighting for the open file and preparing to bring the last piece to the battle. } Ne6 4. Rad1 b4 ( { There was the threat of } 4... b4 4. Bxa7 { , winning a pawn. } ) 
5. Bd5 $1 { The knight on d3 is at last forced to retreat. } Ndc5 6. Nd4 $1 ( { The exchange of knights will have a weakening effect on Black's queenside defence; who can't really avoid it because of the dual threats, after } 6. Nd4 { of } -- 7. Nc6 ( { and } 7. Nxe6 fxe6 8. Bxe6 { - Shereshevsky. } ) 
 ) 
Nxd4 7. Rxd4 Rb8 8. Rb1 Nd3 9. Rb3 $1 Rc7 $2 { In a difficult position, Florian blunders. } 10. Rbxd3 $1 Bxd3 11. Bf4 
