 { Black is under serious pressure in this multi-piece ending, and things got worse after Petrosian's next, powerful move. } 1. { -KEY- } Na5 $1 { Provoking the advance of the b7-pawn, which will leave the enemy queenside badly weakened on the light squares. } b5 ( { After } 1... b6 2. Nc6 Qd6 3. Qxd6 Bxd6 4. Be2 { White also has a strong initiative, with ideas like } b5 5. c4 $1 ) 
2. Nc6 Qe8 ( { Or } 2... Qd6 3. Qxd6 Bxd6 4. Be2 { preparing c2-c4, as in the previous note. } ) 
3. c4 $1 { Another key move, which clears the f1-a6 diagonal towards Black's a-pawn. } Nf6 { The trade of the a6- and e4-pawns won't favour Black; } ( { but he was short of better options; e.g. } 3... Nb8 $2 4. Nxe5 ) 
4. cxb5 axb5 5. Qxb5 Nxe4 6. Qc4 { The passed a-pawn now yields White a decisive advantage. } Nd6 7. Qd5 h5 ( { Equally hopeless is } 7... Nf4 8. Qxe5 Qxc6 9. Bxf4 { etc. } ) 
8. Bxe6 fxe6 9. Qc5 Nf5 10. Qc2 Bg7 11. b5 Nd4 12. Qc4 $1 { Simplest - denying Black any counterplay. } Qd7 13. a4 Nf5 14. Qe2 { Covering the check on d1 just in case. In view of the imminent advance of the a-pawn, Sax resigned. } 
 1-0

